          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            is a
            %em sheaf
            if its value on any object of the site is given by its compatible values on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
            of that object.
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivation+for+sheaves%2C+cohomology+and+higher+stacks"}> motivation for sheaves, cohomology and higher stacks
              \.
          %p
            A competing, though related, definition which one sometimes sees uses “sheaf” as a synonym for
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+space"} étale space
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AsEtaleSpace"} discussion below
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            There are several equivalent ways to characterize sheaves. We start with the general but explicit componentwise definition and then discuss more
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} general abstract
            equivalent reformulations. Finally we give special discussion applicable in various common special cases.
          %h3#GeneralDefinitionInComponents General definition in components
